{"type":"message","id":"76ae5930","parentId":"e39b44e0","timestamp":"2026-05-03T20:41:07.729Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The error name IS \"ZodError\", so my error handler should catch it. But the tests still return 500. This means either:\n1. The API server hasn't restarted and is still running the old code\n2. The ZodError is being caught before it reaches the error handler (maybe Fastify's validation catches it?)\n\nWait, actually let me re-read the route handler:\n\n```ts\nfastify.post('/', { onRequest: [fastify.authenticate] }, async (request, reply) => {\n    const body = CreateProjectBody.parse(request.body);\n    const data = await projectsService.create(body);\n    return reply.status(201).send({ data });\n  });\n```\n\nThe `.parse()` call is inside an async handler. If it throws, Fastify's error handler should catch it. But wait — Fastify wraps route handlers differently. Maybe Fastify catches errors that happen in the handler and calls `setErrorHandler` with them?\n\nActually, looking at the error handler again: it takes `FastifyError | AppError | Error`. When `.parse()` throws a `ZodError`, it's an `Error` instance, so it should go through the error handler. The `error.name === 'ZodError'` check should match.\n\nUnless the error is transformed by Fastify before reaching the error handler?\n\nActually, wait. I just noticed something. In the route, there's no try/catch around `.parse()`. The error propagation through Fastify might be different. Let me check if Fastify's error handler actually receives the error from `.parse()`.\n\nLet me look at how `tsx watch` works. The process listing shows:\n```\n/opt/homebrew/Cellar/node/25.9.0_2/bin/node --require /Users/alessio/.openclaw/workspace/ensoria-api/node_modules/tsx/dist/preflight.cjs --import file:///Users/alessio/.openclaw/workspace/ensoria-api/node_modules/tsx/dist/loader.mjs src/index.ts\n```\n\nThis is running with `tsx` but maybe not in watch mode. Let me check if the process needs to be killed and restarted.\n\nActually wait, looking at the process list again:\n```\nalessio 29708 ... npm exec tsx src/index.ts\nalessio 29723 ... node .../tsx src/index.ts\nalessio 29724 ... node .../tsx/dist/loader.mjs src/index.ts\n```\n\nBut also:\n```\nalessio 24771 ... node .../tsx watch src/index.ts\n```\n\nThere are TWO instances running! One from `tsx watch` (pid 24771) and one from `npm exec tsx src/index.ts` (pid 29708). The one from `npm exec` might be the one listening on port 3000 and it's NOT in watch mode.\n\nLet me kill the duplicate and make sure only the watch mode one is running, or restart the correct one.\n\nActually, let me just kill the non-watch one and let the watch one take over, or restart everything.\n\nHmm, but `tsx watch` might just be watching for changes and re-running, but the process on port 3000 might be the `npm exec tsx src/index.ts` one (which is NOT in watch mode).\n\nLet me kill the extra processes and restart properly.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_17SeSOWgXdxdpWU6t75d7174","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"# Kill the duplicate API processes and restart\nkill 29708 29723 29724 29725 24771 2>/dev/null\nsleep 2\n\n# Check if port 3000 is free\nlsof -i :3000 2>/dev/null | head -5","timeout":10}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":98,"output":824,"cacheRead":67712,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":68634,"cost":{"input":0.00017052000000000001,"output":0.00286752,"cacheRead":0.009818239999999999,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.01285628}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1777840845120,"responseId":"27da35d4-b483-468e-8de8-ff14faf7dc71"}}
